# shellcheck shell=bash
#
# shared/guard.lib -- what a guard's exit status says, for both commit-time
# gates (issue 0720). SOURCED, never run: it is 0644 so `is_handler` (-f AND -x)
# cannot mistake it for a command.
#
# THE ONE READING OF A GUARD'S EXIT STATUS (issue 0714). A guard whose status is
# read runs as `x=0; bash <tool> || x=$?`, never as a bare call: under `set -e`
# a bare call's non-zero exit ends the gate where it stands, before it reports,
# so the commit gate never reaches its later guards or its summary, and a cut
# gate never names the log and the remedy. Its status then means one thing for
# every such guard: 0 passes, 1 is the finding its step names, and anything
# else is no verdict, named by its code and never by a cause it did not
# establish, since a control that did not fire, a tool's own die() and bash's
# 126 and 127 all land there.
#
# BOTH GATES READ A GUARD'S STATUS HERE, AND EACH REPORTS IT ITS OWN WAY. The
# commit gate (cmd/precommit) adds the guard's line to its summary and runs the
# guards after it. A cut gate (cut/gate) refuses at once, naming the log the
# guard wrote and its remedy. Until issue 0720 the cut's contract and reference
# checks each restated this reading in their own `case` arms and their own
# words ("gave no verdict (rc=N)"), so a change to what a status means could
# reach one gate and not the other.

# guard_verdict <status> -- what a guard's exit <status> says, on stdout: `pass`
# for 0, `finding` for 1, and for any other status the one wording of no
# verdict, `gave no verdict (exit <status>)`, which its caller follows with
# where the guard's own output is. A caller reads `pass` and `finding` by name
# and takes anything else as no verdict, so a status this does not know can
# never read as a pass.
guard_verdict() {
  case "$1" in
    0) printf 'pass\n' ;;
    1) printf 'finding\n' ;;
    *) printf 'gave no verdict (exit %s)\n' "$1" ;;
  esac
}
